    "result_text": "The full suite is green \u2014 **36 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in `OwnerRestControllerV1.normalizeTelephone()`.\n\n**Change** (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`): after the existing \"+' followed by 8\u201315 digits\" check, the method now validates the national-number length against the country code where a rule is defined:\n\n- `+61` (Australia) \u2192 the digits after `61` must be exactly **9**\n- `+1` (NANP) \u2192 the digits after `1` must be exactly **10**\n\nA wrong length throws `InvalidTelephoneException`, which the existing `ExceptionControllerAdvice` maps to **400 Bad Request**. Country codes without a defined rule (e.g. `+64`) keep the prior generic 8\u201315 digit behavior.\n\nThe country-code prefixes are unambiguous here: no country code is bare `6`, so `61` uniquely identifies Australia, and `1` is the sole NANP code. This is consistent with the codebase's own conventions \u2014 e.g. `uniqueTelephone()` in `AcceptanceBase` (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 9 national digits after dropping the leading `0`) already documents the \"+61 => 9 national digits\" rule.\n\nCp26 (`+61 123` \u2192 400), Cp08, Cp03, Cp02, and all other acceptance tests pass.",
